{"attack":true,"case":"Corrective Divergence is logically incompatible with the countercondition of a checklist so unambiguous that disagreement is impossible: a checklist that is unambiguous to all competent readers cannot yield the initial disagreement the target requires, so the target's own marker excludes the very case it must cover.\nThe problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere compliance. The target defines Corrective Divergence: initial internal disagreement on checklist items that converges to the correct answer. In the unambiguous-checklist countercondition, every competent reader reaches the same reading; there is no initial disagreement to record. A single evaluation is statically one run, so the observation token cannot produce divergence that the deterministic checklist precludes.\ncites: 4e03191ac5d136feaddae1950e5fc8e05372297e6e0c7cac4a28a151140e1f7e","counterexample":["Checklist item: \"Does the value equal 1? (Yes/No)\"; item value = 1. All panel members read Yes with no divergence.","The target's Corrective Divergence test returns false (no divergence to converge), labeling this mere compliance.","Yet a panel producing exactly the same No->Yes convergence that the target demands would be labeled a genuine reliability gain—identical determinate outputs do not support distinguishing compliance from reliability, and the unambiguous case lacks any initial disagreement to satisfy the divergence criterion."]}